ACC-FIVE pass-2 prototype — PROPOSED re-cut of the section instrument.
r0/research row reported MOVED: `pass2/research/ACC-FIVE/probe/dash-law.mjs`.

The banked instrument states a law the built tree falsifies. It reads `pathLengthAttr` and
`dasharray` into its geometry block and its header names the mechanism as
`totalLength/pathLength` mis-scaling of an attribute-declared dash. Measured on this lane's
prototype (readings/dash-live.json, dash-discriminate.json, dash-why.json, dash-why2.json):

  · with NO pathLength and the dash in real user units, WebKit still reads 4 runs / 0.215 at
    p = 0.05 and 0.970 at p = 0.25;
  · all four declaration arms (attribute dash, CSS dash, period == path, offset 0) are
    identical in WebKit;
  · removing the three inactive poses changes nothing;
  · a plain 4-corner rect is CORRECT (1 run, 0.051), a straight 599-segment rectangle is
    BROKEN (5 runs, 0.258), the 492-segment pose is broken (4 runs), a 123-segment decimation
    of the same pose is correct.

  THE LAW: WebKit restarts a polyline's dash phase roughly every 128 segments. Chromium
  dashes the path whole. `pathLength` is not the bound; the spelling is not the bound; the
  wobble is not the bound. 3.966 was this ring's perimeter, and it sat close enough to 4 to
  look like an explanation.

An instrument that asserts over `pathLength` will green a tree that is still broken — decimate
the pose below 128 and the defect goes quiet while the mechanism is untouched. It must assert
over segment count, and it must carry the geometry arms that isolate it.

